// The engine: the entry into code compiled at run time and the PASM
// templates words are made of, in eng.spin2, whose comments give the
// registers, the stacks and the trap. Its methods are the functions here,
// Spin2 telling no case apart: engRun is PUB engRun.
//
// The engine's state is in registers and LUT RAM of the cog that runs it, so
// a program runs words on one cog only, the one that calls engRun, and the
// accessors here read that cog's registers.

// engRun runs the code at xt until it returns or traps and answers the clocks
// it took. fresh is 1 to start a word and 0 to resume a trap's continuation.
func engRun(xt, fresh uint32) uint32

// engStart runs the code at xt on a free cog with par in PTRA and answers the
// cog, or -1.
func engStart(xt, par uint32) int32

// engTemplates answers the hub address of the first template.
func engTemplates() uint32

// engRefs answers the hub address of the instructions the encoder's test holds
// the compiler's output to, as flexspin assembled them.
func engRefs() uint32

// engMissAddr answers the hub address of the routine that copies a cached loop
// into cog RAM.
func engMissAddr() uint32

func engSetTos(v uint32)
func engGetTos() uint32
func engSetDsp(v uint32)
func engGetDsp() uint32
func engSetRp(v uint32)
func engGetRp() uint32
func engGetPb() uint32
func engSetPb(v uint32)

// engRequest answers a trap's request, 0 when the word returned.
func engRequest() uint32

// engContinuation answers where a trapped word goes on.
func engContinuation() uint32

// engLayout answers the stacks' bounds: 0 for the data stack's base, 1 for its
// end, 2 for the empty return stack.
func engLayout(i int32) uint32
